WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.552
sigfpe's Derivative

00:00:01.364 --> 00:00:02.012
flipping

00:00:02.142 --> 00:00:03.569
polynomial pairings

00:00:03.381 --> 00:00:05.408
for automatic differentiation

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:07.419
Dan Piponi borrows

00:00:07.231 --> 00:00:08.528
the Brzozowski derivative

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.566
idea from regex

00:00:09.377 --> 00:00:10.462
matching to define

00:00:10.274 --> 00:00:11.594
a polynomial pairing

00:00:11.406 --> 00:00:12.231
in Haskell

00:00:12.766 --> 00:00:13.791
It is symmetric

00:00:13.686 --> 00:00:14.440
so the roles

00:00:14.252 --> 00:00:15.171
can be swapped

00:00:15.018 --> 00:00:16.398
and intermediate results

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:17.105
stay small

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:18.756
a 2D Laplacian

00:00:18.662 --> 00:00:19.853
needs only 4

00:00:19.665 --> 00:00:20.843
tracked coefficients

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:21.457
instead of the

00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:22.141
full Hessian

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:23.955
The post generalizes

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:24.875
the author's earlier

00:00:24.686 --> 00:00:25.736
Laplacian trick

00:00:25.913 --> 00:00:27.068
later optimized in

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:27.976
folx and used

00:00:27.788 --> 00:00:28.766
for PINNs and

00:00:28.578 --> 00:00:29.368
VMC

00:00:29.569 --> 00:00:31.219
to any constant-coefficient

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:32.304
differential operator

00:00:32.293 --> 00:00:32.941
such as the

00:00:32.753 --> 00:00:33.837
biharmonic

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:35.364
Full story on

00:00:35.175 --> 00:00:36.071
the dashboard
